📅 Date Table in Power BI (CALENDARAUTO vs CALENDAR)
Why Date Table?

If your data contains date columns, creating a Date Table makes:

Filtering easy

Time intelligence (YTD, MTD, YoY) work properly

Relationships clean and correct

🟢 Method 1: Using CALENDARAUTO()
Steps

Go to Modeling

Click New Table

Write in formula bar:

Calendar = CALENDARAUTO()


✅ A new table named Calendar will be created
✅ It appears on the right side in Tables pane

How CALENDARAUTO() Works

Automatically scans all date columns in the model

Finds the earliest and latest date

Creates a continuous date table

📌 No need to mention start date and end date

When to Use CALENDARAUTO()

✔ Small or medium datasets
✔ Quick setup
✔ When all date columns are reliable

⚠️ Not recommended if:

Your model has unwanted date columns

You need full control on date range

🟢 Method 2: Using CALENDAR()
Steps

Go to Modeling

Click New Table

Write:

Calendar =
CALENDAR(
    MIN(Data[Accident_Date]),
    MAX(Data[Accident_Date])
)

How CALENDAR() Works

You manually define start and end date

Uses only the required date column

Creates date rows from MIN date to MAX date

When to Use CALENDAR()

✔ Large datasets
✔ Production reports
✔ Full control over date range

🟡 Creating Date Columns (Derived Columns)

After creating Date Table, you can add columns:

Year
Year = YEAR(Calendar[Date])

Month Number
Month No = MONTH(Calendar[Date])

Month Name
Month Name = FORMAT(Calendar[Date], "MMMM")

🔗 Important Final Step

Mark Calendar as Date Table

Create relationship:

Calendar[Date] → Data[Accident_Date]

